  # Review: aes-siv 0.7.0
  
  - Upstream: https://github.com/RustCrypto/AEADs (aes-siv)
  - Upstream tag: aes-siv-v0.7.0 (commit 6571311)
  - Verification: pass (source bit-for-bit identical at tag)
  
  ## Code review findings
  
  **Thoroughness: high** (~590 LoC, 2 files, every function examined.)
  
  Pure Rust `#![no_std]` implementation of RFC 5297 AES-SIV. From the RustCrypto project.
  
  ### Non-negotiable checks
  
  - **build.rs**: None. Positive.
  - **Proc macros**: None. Positive.
  - **Dependencies**: All RustCrypto ecosystem (aead, aes, cipher, cmac, ctr, dbl, digest,
    zeroize). All justified.
  - **FFI**: None. Positive.
  - **`unsafe`**: Zero. Positive. All crypto ops use safe RustCrypto trait abstractions.
  
  ### Red-flag scan
  
  Clean. No unsafe, no I/O, no network, no env, no process spawning, no FFI. Pure
  `#![no_std]` computation library.
  
  ### Architecture
  
  Two layers: `Siv<C, M>` (core RFC 5297 implementation) and `SivAead<C, M>` (AEAD
  wrapper). Key split: first half → MAC, second half → AES-CTR encryption. `s2v` function
  implements the vectorized PRF per RFC 5297 Section 2.4.
  
  ### Cryptographic correctness
  
  - **Constant-time tag comparison** via `CtOutput` — prevents timing side-channels.
  - **Key zeroization** on `Drop` — prevents key leakage after use.
  - **Re-encryption on auth failure** — prevents leaking unauthenticated plaintext.
  - **IV bit clearing** (`iv[8] &= 0x7f; iv[12] &= 0x7f`) — matches RFC 5297 spec.
  - **MAX_HEADERS (126)** check in `s2v` — per RFC limit.
  - Test vectors from RFC 5297 and Wycheproof in `tests/` directory.
  
  ### Reconciliation against claims
  
  Does exactly what it claims. Pure computation, no I/O, all deps from RustCrypto.
  
  ## Cross-check
  
  No prior reviews at any version.
  
  ## Rating rationale
  
  Positive (not strong because crypto code correctness ultimately requires formal
  verification, and this is an LLM review). Zero unsafe, correct security practices,
  well-structured RFC implementation.
